I brought something for you as well.

1556
01:01:52,322 --> 01:01:53,481
- Really? - I brought something...

1557
01:01:53,482 --> 01:01:54,821
- Oh, really? - for her.

1558
01:01:54,822 --> 01:01:58,791
- I'm excited. - The box was too big,

1559
01:01:58,792 --> 01:02:00,292
so I brought them without the box.

1560
01:02:00,592 --> 01:02:02,532
I've tried using these.

1561
01:02:04,092 --> 01:02:06,762
- Are these leg massagers? - Yes.

1562
01:02:08,932 --> 01:02:10,032
- Gosh! - This one.

1563
01:02:10,402 --> 01:02:12,571
This one is shaped like a fork.

1564
01:02:12,572 --> 01:02:14,542
- Oh, my gosh! - It works like this.

1565
01:02:14,802 --> 01:02:16,012
Just like this.

1566
01:02:17,042 --> 01:02:18,712
- Mothers love those. - Gosh.

1567
01:02:19,842 --> 01:02:21,011
(The daughter-in-law loves it as well.)

1568
01:02:21,012 --> 01:02:22,152
It's nice, isn't it?

1569
01:02:22,682 --> 01:02:23,882
The daughter-in-law loves it even more.

1570
01:02:24,252 --> 01:02:26,752
- It feels great. - We will be making kimchi today.

1571
01:02:27,222 --> 01:02:29,751
It'll be for the family. Are you really going to help?

1572
01:02:29,752 --> 01:02:31,591
- Yes. Of course. - Gosh.

1573
01:02:31,592 --> 01:02:34,092
We collect the ingredients from the garden.

1574
01:02:34,492 --> 01:02:36,392
There will be a lot of things to do.

1575
01:02:36,532 --> 01:02:38,931
They collect all of the ingredients from the garden.

1576
01:02:38,932 --> 01:02:40,062
- The vegetable garden? - Yes.

1577
01:02:40,802 --> 01:02:42,802
- I tried making kimchi. - Gosh, that goes a while back.

1578
01:02:43,832 --> 01:02:46,372
(He even made oyster radish kimchi...)

1579
01:02:47,202 --> 01:02:50,012
(and captured Kany's appetite.)

1580
01:02:50,612 --> 01:02:52,712
(He swiftly made a large kimchi dish as well.)

1581
01:02:53,312 --> 01:02:55,352
- You got some of that as well. - That was really good.

1582
01:02:56,552 --> 01:02:58,551
As I only received things from her,

1583
01:02:58,552 --> 01:03:00,522
I wanted to return her the favor.

1584
01:03:00,682 --> 01:03:03,222
I decided to help her out with kimchi making,

1585
01:03:03,522 --> 01:03:06,761
and I also wanted to learn the secret of her great kimchi.

1586
01:03:06,762 --> 01:03:08,362
That was why I decided to visit.

1587
01:03:09,832 --> 01:03:11,662
- Let's get changed, - Right.

1588
01:03:12,062 --> 01:03:14,101
- so that we can work diligently. - Okay.

1589
01:03:14,102 --> 01:03:15,401
That's such a fascinating relationship.

1590
01:03:15,402 --> 01:03:16,471
Right?

1591
01:03:16,472 --> 01:03:17,631
(Laughing)

1592
01:03:17,632 --> 01:03:18,672
Right.

1593
01:03:18,842 --> 01:03:20,901
It feels like space and time are distorted.

1594
01:03:20,902 --> 01:03:21,941
(That's how fascinating it is.)

1595
01:03:21,942 --> 01:03:22,942
Right?

1596
01:03:22,943 --> 01:03:24,772
- Let's head out to the garden now. - Right.

1597
01:03:27,582 --> 01:03:29,282
Go out and do a lot of work today.

1598
01:03:31,682 --> 01:03:33,681
- It sounds exciting. - It does, doesn't it?

1599
01:03:33,682 --> 01:03:35,951
I have never done this before.

1600
01:03:35,952 --> 01:03:37,052
Will I be able to do this?

1601
01:03:37,552 --> 01:03:38,962
Will I be able to do this, Mom?

1602
01:03:40,162 --> 01:03:41,891
(Her heart pounds as it's her first time making kimchi.)

1603
01:03:41,892 --> 01:03:43,592
- Is everything there? - Yes.

1604
01:03:44,762 --> 01:03:46,302
Gosh, they're holding hands.

1605
01:03:46,532 --> 01:03:49,032
- My goodness. - The weather is so nice!

1606
01:03:50,232 --> 01:03:51,301
This must be it.

1607
01:03:51,302 --> 01:03:53,002
- Here. - Really?

1608
01:03:53,102 --> 01:03:55,142
- Be careful, Mom! - I'm fine.

1609
01:03:55,742 --> 01:03:57,412
- Are you okay? - Gosh!

1610
01:03:57,572 --> 01:03:59,341
- This is it. - Gosh!

1611
01:03:59,342 --> 01:04:00,442
It's a vegetable garden right next to the apartment complex.

1612
01:04:01,142 --> 01:04:02,312
Let's take a look.

1613
01:04:03,382 --> 01:04:04,612
It's quite big.

1614
01:04:07,722 --> 01:04:09,291
(It's full of crops that will be used for kimchi.)

1615
01:04:09,292 --> 01:04:10,322
She's a rich farmer.

1616
01:04:10,452 --> 01:04:12,692
- She doesn't own the whole area. - Is that so?

1617
01:04:13,022 --> 01:04:15,161
Parts of the garden must be owned by different people.

1618
01:04:15,162 --> 01:04:16,662
Yes, that's right.

1619
01:04:16,992 --> 01:04:18,091
What should we start with? This

1620
01:04:18,092 --> 01:04:21,061
The radishes must be collected and sorted out.

1621
01:04:21,062 --> 01:04:23,132
These are the ones that will be used for the kimchi filling.

1622
01:04:24,532 --> 01:04:25,742
They're totally organic.

1623
01:04:26,242 --> 01:04:27,302
How many of these are needed?

1624
01:04:27,672 --> 01:04:29,811
- These? Around 15? - How many?

1625
01:04:29,812 --> 01:04:30,812
- Around 15? - Around 15?

1626
01:04:30,812 --> 01:04:31,812
There are some at home.

1627
01:04:32,112 --> 01:04:34,541
- Three, oh! - Okay.

1628
01:04:34,542 --> 01:04:36,612
And four, oh!

1629
01:04:36,882 --> 01:04:39,021
- And 5, oh! - And 5, oh!

1630
01:04:39,022 --> 01:04:41,382
- Kany is bringing up her energy. - And six, oh!

1631
01:04:41,552 --> 01:04:43,351
- And 7, oh! - And 7.

1632
01:04:43,352 --> 01:04:44,991
And eight, oh!

1633
01:04:44,992 --> 01:04:46,792
Hey! And eight, oh!

1634
01:04:47,222 --> 01:04:50,132
You feel energized when there's a friend like Kany.

1635
01:04:50,362 --> 01:04:52,601
And 12, oh!

1636
01:04:52,602 --> 01:04:54,202
Thirteen, oh!

1637
01:04:54,302 --> 01:04:56,231
- No more! - Fourteen, oh!

1638
01:04:56,232 --> 01:04:58,571
- She looks exhausted. - We need only 15. Stop!

1639
01:04:58,572 --> 01:05:00,102
- That's 15. - That's enough!

1640
01:05:01,802 --> 01:05:03,842
These should be moved over there.

1641
01:05:04,442 --> 01:05:06,582
Collect the big ones from here.

1642
01:05:06,682 --> 01:05:07,782
The big ones?

1643
01:05:08,712 --> 01:05:10,612
(He watches the master demonstrate.)

1644
01:05:10,652 --> 01:05:12,152
- Like that? - Yes.

1645
01:05:13,282 --> 01:05:15,352
You two are doing well at your first attempts.

1646
01:05:15,722 --> 01:05:18,752
- Well, I had my own garden too. - Right.

1647
01:05:21,522 --> 01:05:23,092
(Their hands get used to the garden work.)

1648
01:05:23,592 --> 01:05:26,261
One person should pick out the radish like this.

1649
01:05:26,262 --> 01:05:27,331
- Right. - Right. Gosh.

1650
01:05:27,332 --> 01:05:28,932
The rest will sort the radishes out.

1651
01:05:29,272 --> 01:05:31,272
- Gosh. - Just do it like this.

1652
01:05:31,502 --> 01:05:33,171
It's really easy. Okay.

1653
01:05:33,172 --> 01:05:34,241
Oh, my gosh.

1654
01:05:34,242 --> 01:05:35,742
(The young radish is picked out swiftly.)

1655
01:05:36,472 --> 01:05:37,742
- Gosh. - Your legs.

1656
01:05:37,872 --> 01:05:39,382
- My back. - Sit here and do it.

1657
01:05:40,242 --> 01:05:42,551
I told you I had a disc problem.

1658
01:05:42,552 --> 01:05:44,212
- Oh, yes. - Kany can't have her legs crossed.

1659
01:05:44,512 --> 01:05:45,981
- Do it slowly. - Right.

1660
01:05:45,982 --> 01:05:47,081
- It will be exhausting. - Yes!

1661
01:05:47,082 --> 01:05:48,151
I like it!

1662
01:05:48,152 --> 01:05:50,152
Right? Sit down and work.

1663
01:05:51,422 --> 01:05:53,222
(Sitting down)

1664
01:05:53,922 --> 01:05:55,861
It's nice as the weather is great, isn't it?

1665
01:05:55,862 --> 01:05:57,332
- It's wonderful. - It's wonderful.

1666
01:05:58,092 --> 01:05:59,532
Everything looks fascinating.

1667
01:06:01,062 --> 01:06:02,372
It's a stress-reliever.

1668
01:06:06,072 --> 01:06:08,042
- Like so and so. - Right.

1669
01:06:08,302 --> 01:06:09,411
Gosh, this one is big!

1670
01:06:09,412 --> 01:06:11,172
- These parts must be sorted out. - Wait.

1671
01:06:12,382 --> 01:06:14,212
- Hold on. - This is good enough, right?

1672
01:06:14,482 --> 01:06:16,612
- Yes. - This isn't too small, right?

1673
01:06:20,552 --> 01:06:22,082
You're good at this.

1674
01:06:23,052 --> 01:06:24,452
(Carving)

1675
01:06:28,162 --> 01:06:29,192
Kany!

1676
01:06:32,862 --> 01:06:35,432
Okay. Like this.

1677
01:06:35,802 --> 01:06:37,032
Is this okay?

1678
01:06:37,972 --> 01:06:40,602
- So this is what Mother did. - Okay.

1679
01:06:40,802 --> 01:06:42,571
Think of this as a sample.

1680
01:06:42,572 --> 01:06:44,242
- This is what she did. - Okay.

1681
01:06:45,142 --> 01:06:47,082
Oh, no. Well, yes.

1682
01:06:47,882 --> 01:06:48,952
(She finally puts in one.)

1683
01:06:49,782 --> 01:06:50,982
Do it quick!

1684
01:06:51,982 --> 01:06:53,622
(She quietly picks up the radish Kany worked on.)

1685
01:06:54,282 --> 01:06:55,952
(She works on it again without Kany knowing.)

1686
01:06:58,322 --> 01:07:00,792
I love doing that. I think I have a talent.

1687
01:07:01,262 --> 01:07:02,462
I'm serious.

1688
01:07:02,692 --> 01:07:04,561
Are you going to switch jobs?

1689
01:07:04,562 --> 01:07:06,562
Are you going to stay with me now?

1690
01:07:06,962 --> 01:07:09,172
This is so much fun. Why is this so fun to do?

1691
01:07:09,472 --> 01:07:10,972
- Are you having fun? - Yes!

1692
01:07:11,872 --> 01:07:14,942
It felt awkward just at the start. You're loving it here now, right?

1693
01:07:15,642 --> 01:07:17,171
Father was so shy.

1694
01:07:17,172 --> 01:07:18,271
He's really introverted.

1695
01:07:18,272 --> 01:07:19,782
He must've felt awkward at first.

1696
01:07:19,882 --> 01:07:21,512
I heard he talks about Kany at work as well.

1697
01:07:22,512 --> 01:07:24,681
He says he got a good daughter-in-law.

1698
01:07:24,682 --> 01:07:25,782
I know.

1699
01:07:26,122 --> 01:07:27,622
- He boasts about you. - Really?

1700
01:07:27,722 --> 01:07:30,091
He says a lot of good things about you at work.

1701
01:07:30,092 --> 01:07:31,221
She's worth boasting about.

1702
01:07:31,222 --> 01:07:32,462
- Mom! - Yes?

1703
01:07:32,792 --> 01:07:34,192
- From now on, - Yes?

1704
01:07:34,462 --> 01:07:36,031
- I will visit often. - Will you help me out often?

1705
01:07:36,032 --> 01:07:37,332
- Really? - Will you come to help me?

1706
01:07:37,632 --> 01:07:38,632
She's so cute.

1707
01:07:38,633 --> 01:07:40,002
(I'm so grateful.)

1708
01:07:40,102 --> 01:07:42,002
What will we eat today?

1709
01:07:42,402 --> 01:07:43,872
Today? Bossam.

1710
01:07:44,032 --> 01:07:45,802
Oyster bossam!

1711
01:07:46,172 --> 01:07:47,341
- What? - Yes.

1712
01:07:47,342 --> 01:07:49,112
- She loves oyster bossam. - Really?

1713
01:07:49,472 --> 01:07:51,312
I must make you your favorite.

1714
01:07:52,142 --> 01:07:54,211
- That's your favorite. - I love you, Mom.

1715
01:07:54,212 --> 01:07:55,812
- Okay. Let's make it quick! - Let's hurry!

1716
01:07:56,082 --> 01:07:57,352
- Let's hurry! - Let's hurry!

1717
01:07:58,512 --> 01:08:00,552
(Singing gibberish)

1718
01:08:01,122 --> 01:08:03,052
(Mulkimchi!)

1719
01:08:03,392 --> 01:08:04,822
(This rhythm feels familiar.)

1720
01:08:05,522 --> 01:08:08,061
(Your irresistible spell)

1721
01:08:08,062 --> 01:08:09,532
(Mulkimchi)

1722
01:08:09,692 --> 01:08:10,692
This is it.

1723
01:08:11,362 --> 01:08:12,902
- It was this song? - No wonder it sounded familiar!

1724
01:08:15,872 --> 01:08:18,132
Mulkimchi, in freedom

1725
01:08:18,302 --> 01:08:19,972
(Singing gibberish)

1726
01:08:20,202 --> 01:08:21,642
(Singing gibberish)

1727
01:08:21,872 --> 01:08:23,442
She's doing whatever she wishes to with my song.

1728
01:08:23,772 --> 01:08:24,872
(Radish mic)

1729
01:08:25,442 --> 01:08:26,782
So that's what it sounds like to her?

1730
01:08:28,242 --> 01:08:29,552
(Scraping)

1731
01:08:30,412 --> 01:08:32,421
(Her hands move quickly with the mulkimchi song beat.)

1732
01:08:32,422 --> 01:08:34,152
There's even more work to do.

1733
01:08:34,222 --> 01:08:35,322
They're picking up the pace.

1734
01:08:35,852 --> 01:08:37,452
Gosh, I love her energy.

1735
01:08:40,862 --> 01:08:41,922
They worked on a lot of them.

1736
01:08:42,562 --> 01:08:43,992
- Mom! - Yes?

1737
01:08:44,392 --> 01:08:46,362
I worked on all of these.

1738
01:08:46,662 --> 01:08:48,532
- Great job! - Great job!

1739
01:08:48,732 --> 01:08:50,102
You're the best!

1740
01:08:51,602 --> 01:08:53,172
Come and work on these as well.

1741
01:08:53,842 --> 01:08:55,501
- Okay. These are done. - Right.

1742
01:08:55,502 --> 01:08:57,141
(They collected the ingredients for around two hours.)

1743
01:08:57,142 --> 01:08:58,572
- Gosh! - Great work.

1744
01:08:59,542 --> 01:09:00,542
(Gosh!)

1745
01:09:00,612 --> 01:09:02,242
- Do you feel exhausted? - I'm fine.

1746
01:09:02,512 --> 01:09:03,652
You feel okay?

1747
01:09:03,782 --> 01:09:05,251
We still have a lot more to do.

1748
01:09:05,252 --> 01:09:06,552
Where else can you find a daughter-in-law like that?

1749
01:09:06,882 --> 01:09:09,092
Making kimchi is tough, even when the ingredients are bought.

1750
01:09:09,552 --> 01:09:11,691
- They all have to be worked on. - Don't worry. It's okay.

1751
01:09:11,692 --> 01:09:13,722
It's okay. We'll have oyster bossam.

1752
01:09:14,462 --> 01:09:15,831
(They're done harvesting. They'll start making kimchi now.)

1753
01:09:15,832 --> 01:09:18,292
Oh, boy. What do we do? We need to hurry up.

1754
01:09:18,432 --> 01:09:20,361
We should move them to the veranda.

1755
01:09:20,362 --> 01:09:21,832
- To the veranda? Okay. - Yes.

1756
01:09:24,232 --> 01:09:26,001
- The napa cabbage are salted. - How many heads are they?

1757
01:09:26,002 --> 01:09:29,002
Oh, my. It looks like a side dish store.

1758
01:09:29,712 --> 01:09:32,842
(The plastic basins are filled with salted napa cabbage.)

1759
01:09:33,382 --> 01:09:34,682
That's a lot.

1760
01:09:34,842 --> 01:09:36,451
When she makes kimchi alone,

1761
01:09:36,452 --> 01:09:38,311
she uses 50 or 60 heads of napa cabbage.

1762
01:09:38,312 --> 01:09:39,551
Then did you make more kimchi than that?

1763
01:09:39,552 --> 01:09:40,852
We used 70 heads of napa cabbage.

1764
01:09:42,222 --> 01:09:44,052
(Amazed)

1765
01:09:44,322 --> 01:09:46,721
These napa cabbage are in a different color, right?

1766
01:09:46,722 --> 01:09:48,361
- Yes, they're yellow. - Right.

1767
01:09:48,362 --> 01:09:50,631
These are not regular napa cabbage.

1768
01:09:50,632 --> 01:09:52,061
They're tastier and more refreshing.

1769
01:09:52,062 --> 01:09:54,662
- That's why they're yellow. - Yes. They look different, right?

1770
01:09:55,562 --> 01:09:57,732
It's a different type of napa cabbage.

1771
01:09:57,902 --> 01:10:00,472
The napa cabbage we know...

1772
01:10:00,602 --> 01:10:02,711
has stems, the white part, in the center.

1773
01:10:02,712 --> 01:10:04,611
And the leafy part is light yellow...

1774
01:10:04,612 --> 01:10:07,182
or green. You can see a gradient of those colors.

1775
01:10:07,312 --> 01:10:10,952
But most of the leafy part of this type was yellow.

1776
01:10:11,012 --> 01:10:12,352
The yellow type tastes good.

1777
01:10:12,622 --> 01:10:14,722
She grew the napa cabbage by herself.

1778
01:10:15,022 --> 01:10:17,521
She said that a small amount of salt was enough...

1779
01:10:17,522 --> 01:10:19,461
to salt napa cabbage...

1780
01:10:19,462 --> 01:10:21,922
if you salted them for two days.

1781
01:10:22,092 --> 01:10:24,431
And that makes kimchi taste even better.

1782
01:10:24,432 --> 01:10:26,231
The napa cabbage absorb the kimchi sauce well too.

1783
01:10:26,232 --> 01:10:27,501
- Oh, I didn't know that. - I see.

1784
01:10:27,502 --> 01:10:29,771
They do look different from the ones in stores.

1785
01:10:29,772 --> 01:10:31,071
- They're different. - It feels different.

1786
01:10:31,072 --> 01:10:33,401
- They're salted well. - It's tasty as it is.

1787
01:10:33,402 --> 01:10:35,812
- It's not too salty. - The saltiness must be just right.

1788
01:10:36,342 --> 01:10:39,311
Shall we make dried pollack porridge?

1789
01:10:39,312 --> 01:10:40,441
- Dried pollack porridge? - Yes.

1790
01:10:40,442 --> 01:10:41,482
Dried pollack porridge.

1791
01:10:41,742 --> 01:10:42,782
Dried pollack porridge?

1792
01:10:43,012 --> 01:10:45,952
We use dried pollack to make kimchi.

1793
01:10:46,822 --> 01:10:47,822
Kany.

1794
01:10:47,823 --> 01:10:50,151
Can you wash the radish...

1795
01:10:50,152 --> 01:10:52,322
in front of the salted cabbage?

1796
01:10:53,322 --> 01:10:54,862
Okay.

1797
01:10:54,892 --> 01:10:57,232
Put these on when you're out there.

1798
01:10:57,492 --> 01:10:59,661
- Oh, okay. - Be sure your feet don't get wet.

1799
01:10:59,662 --> 01:11:01,762
- Okay. - Okay. Be careful.

1800
01:11:01,862 --> 01:11:03,231
- Kany. - Yes?

1801
01:11:03,232 --> 01:11:04,472
I know you can do it.

1802
01:11:06,172 --> 01:11:08,042
(Rubbing)

1803
01:11:09,072 --> 01:11:10,242
Oh, that's fun.

1804
01:11:10,612 --> 01:11:11,912
Oh, that's fun.

1805
01:11:12,982 --> 01:11:14,012
Fun!

1806
01:11:15,242 --> 01:11:16,712
(She finds Kany cute.)

1807
01:11:16,852 --> 01:11:19,081
A, the, wash it

1808
01:11:19,082 --> 01:11:21,221
Wash it

1809
01:11:21,222 --> 01:11:23,622
Come on, radish

1810
01:11:24,152 --> 01:11:26,092
(While washing the radish, she starts singing cheerfully again.)

1811
01:11:27,162 --> 01:11:29,292
Wash the radish, all right

1812
01:11:30,062 --> 01:11:31,792
(Oh, my. Kany never lets me down.)

1813
01:11:32,832 --> 01:11:34,231
(KEY comes back to the kitchen to make dried pollack porridge.)

1814
01:11:34,232 --> 01:11:35,402
Dried pollack porridge?

1815
01:11:36,232 --> 01:11:37,801
(He looks around the kitchen of the master in fascination.)

1816
01:11:37,802 --> 01:11:40,102
That was really fascinating. I'd never seen it before.

1817
01:11:40,702 --> 01:11:43,842
I use this to add sweetness.

1818
01:11:44,142 --> 01:11:45,842
- You add jujube. - Yes.

1819
01:11:46,642 --> 01:11:48,642
(She adds dried shiitake mushroom as well.)

1820
01:11:50,652 --> 01:11:52,181
(Taking dried pollack out)

1821
01:11:52,182 --> 01:11:53,182
Now, the dried pollack...

1822
01:11:53,183 --> 01:11:54,381
You don't make rice flour paste?

1823
01:11:54,382 --> 01:11:56,851
I'll put the paste in the porridge.

1824
01:11:56,852 --> 01:11:59,052
- You'll add it to the porridge? - Yes.

1825
01:11:59,222 --> 01:12:01,322
- She sounds like a kimchi master. - Exactly.

1826
01:12:02,362 --> 01:12:04,192
(Thinking)

1827
01:12:04,562 --> 01:12:06,662
Is this a family recipe?

1828
01:12:06,962 --> 01:12:08,431
- No, it isn't. - No?

1829
01:12:08,432 --> 01:12:10,501
- I invented my own recipe. - You invented it?

1830
01:12:10,502 --> 01:12:11,832
Yes, I did.

1831
01:12:12,902 --> 01:12:15,701
- And it turned out great. - Right. Your kimchi was very tasty.

1832
01:12:15,702 --> 01:12:17,411
Other people say my kimchi is good.

1833
01:12:17,412 --> 01:12:19,381
I guess she made her own recipe from her experience.

1834
01:12:19,382 --> 01:12:20,911
- So she didn't learn that. - She invented the recipe.

1835
01:12:20,912 --> 01:12:22,411
- She invented the recipe. - Yes.

1836
01:12:22,412 --> 01:12:24,351
Three would be enough for one pot.

1837
01:12:24,352 --> 01:12:25,452
(Getting closer to her)

1838
01:12:25,582 --> 01:12:27,152
So you remove those parts.

1839
01:12:27,852 --> 01:12:32,622
(I can't miss any step of the process.)

1840
01:12:33,722 --> 01:12:34,722
(She puts the cleaned dried pollack in the water.)

1841
01:12:34,723 --> 01:12:37,092
The fish will bend when the water gets hot.

1842
01:12:38,132 --> 01:12:39,532
Oh, my. This is very unique.

1843
01:12:40,702 --> 01:12:41,801
(Jujube, shiitake mushroom, onion, kelp, garlic, ginger)

1844
01:12:41,802 --> 01:12:43,572
She uses many different ingredients.

1845
01:12:44,832 --> 01:12:47,072
(Now they're waiting until the broth is ready.)

1846
01:12:48,772 --> 01:12:50,672
- Oh, my. - What?

1847
01:12:52,110 --> 01:12:53,650
It looks like a swimming pool.

1848
01:12:54,210 --> 01:12:55,949
- It looks like a swimming pool. - A swimming pool?

1849
01:12:55,950 --> 01:12:57,020
Yes.

1850
01:12:57,320 --> 01:12:59,320
(Acting like she's swimming)

1851
01:12:59,550 --> 01:13:01,349
Now we should put on gloves.

1852
01:13:01,350 --> 01:13:02,760
We should put on a hat and everything, right?

1853
01:13:02,960 --> 01:13:03,989
Here you go, Kany.

1854
01:13:03,990 --> 01:13:05,289
- Yes. - Yes?

1855
01:13:05,290 --> 01:13:07,460
(Hygiene is essential.)

1856
01:13:08,760 --> 01:13:11,059
(They put on hygienic cooking hats...)

1857
01:13:11,060 --> 01:13:12,499
(and masks.)

1858
01:13:12,500 --> 01:13:14,970
(Clean and hygienic)

1859
01:13:15,170 --> 01:13:16,640
Now we'll use the porridge...

1860
01:13:16,800 --> 01:13:18,140
- We'll use the porridge. - Yes.

1861
01:13:19,040 --> 01:13:20,110
Is this salt?

1862
01:13:20,340 --> 01:13:23,340
It's glutinous rice flour. I'll mix it with water.

1863
01:13:23,840 --> 01:13:24,910
Okay.

1864
01:13:25,010 --> 01:13:27,179
(Stirring)

1865
01:13:27,180 --> 01:13:28,449
Oh, is it done?

1866
01:13:28,450 --> 01:13:29,749
(The glutinous rice paste goes into the dried pollack porridge.)

1867
01:13:29,750 --> 01:13:31,279
- In the broth you made earlier? - Pour it all.

1868
01:13:31,280 --> 01:13:32,820
We put it in the broth.

1869
01:13:33,450 --> 01:13:34,919
- It became sticky. - Right.

1870
01:13:34,920 --> 01:13:37,019
It must be. Now we should bring it to a boil.

1871
01:13:37,020 --> 01:13:38,060
I see.

1872
01:13:38,120 --> 01:13:40,829
- Making kimchi is like... - It's like a whole... Yes.

1873
01:13:40,830 --> 01:13:42,030
It's like a whole journey.

1874
01:13:43,030 --> 01:13:44,959
You can do it

1875
01:13:44,960 --> 01:13:46,499
- Come on - Good. Kany's singing...

1876
01:13:46,500 --> 01:13:47,999
helps it boil faster.

1877
01:13:48,000 --> 01:13:50,240
See? It's coming... It's going to start boiling quickly.

1878
01:13:50,270 --> 01:13:51,299
Come on

1879
01:13:51,300 --> 01:13:53,309
(The daughter-in-law starts singing a work song.)

1880
01:13:53,310 --> 01:13:54,569
(Smiling happily)

1881
01:13:54,570 --> 01:13:56,910
Hey, come on, come on

1882
01:13:57,280 --> 01:13:59,510
(Tapping rhythmically)

1883
01:13:59,750 --> 01:14:01,710
The density is perfect.

1884
01:14:01,820 --> 01:14:05,020
- This is why you call it porridge. - That's right.

1885
01:14:05,080 --> 01:14:06,349
The density is just right.

1886
01:14:06,350 --> 01:14:07,690
Oh, my. The color is fascinating.

1887
01:14:07,790 --> 01:14:08,920
I'm the master of making this.

1888
01:14:09,960 --> 01:14:12,090
- What was that? - Mama is the best.

1889
01:14:16,500 --> 01:14:17,529
What is that?

1890
01:14:17,530 --> 01:14:18,670
(A mysterious machine appears?)

1891
01:14:18,900 --> 01:14:19,900
Good.

1892
01:14:19,901 --> 01:14:20,929
(She keeps bringing equipment even after that.)

1893
01:14:20,930 --> 01:14:22,499
If you put it in here, it will fall on the mat.

1894
01:14:22,500 --> 01:14:25,710
- Oh, my. Look at that. - She has a lot of equipment.

1895
01:14:27,570 --> 01:14:29,010
(Looking carefully)

1896
01:14:29,580 --> 01:14:31,239
I'll do that.

1897
01:14:31,240 --> 01:14:33,150
- You have to press it with this. - Got it.

1898
01:14:34,550 --> 01:14:36,620
(Amazed)

1899
01:14:37,350 --> 01:14:38,350
Oh, my.

1900
01:14:38,550 --> 01:14:39,889
You can julienne radish like that?

1901
01:14:39,890 --> 01:14:41,090
- I can do it from here. - Okay.

1902
01:14:41,150 --> 01:14:42,960
That's easy work.

1903
01:14:43,890 --> 01:14:45,959
- You just have to put it in. - Okay.

1904
01:14:45,960 --> 01:14:48,360
- Now press it. - Oh, my.

1905
01:14:49,900 --> 01:14:51,599
- It juliennes vegetables. - Is there such a machine?

1906
01:14:51,600 --> 01:14:52,630
That's awesome.

1907
01:14:52,670 --> 01:14:53,699
It's like a meat grinder at a butcher shop.

1908
01:14:53,700 --> 01:14:54,900
Right, the machine at a butcher shop.

1909
01:14:55,640 --> 01:14:58,439
- Okay! - I love this work.

1910
01:14:58,440 --> 01:15:00,439
- Yes. - It's fun, isn't it?

1911
01:15:00,440 --> 01:15:02,840
- People used a slicer before. - Right, they used a slicer.

1912
01:15:03,310 --> 01:15:04,579
That's amazing.

1913
01:15:04,580 --> 01:15:06,580
(The amazing slicer)

1914
01:15:06,680 --> 01:15:09,150
Men, use tools.

1915
01:15:10,550 --> 01:15:11,779
(Pressing hard)

1916
01:15:11,780 --> 01:15:13,819
Oh, you have to press? Okay, I'll press it.

1917
01:15:13,820 --> 01:15:15,049
- Now you're doing well. - I'll press it.

1918
01:15:15,050 --> 01:15:17,690
Press it, press it, Girl, press it, press it

1919
01:15:18,320 --> 01:15:22,060
(Her excitement is at the maximum level.)

1920
01:15:22,930 --> 01:15:24,399
(KEY is also fully satisfied.)

1921
01:15:24,400 --> 01:15:25,670
I love it.

1922
01:15:26,300 --> 01:15:28,670
(While pressing it with the cheery energy...)

1923
01:15:29,100 --> 01:15:31,139
We're done with the radish.

1924
01:15:31,140 --> 01:15:32,570
My, they use a lot of radish.

1925
01:15:35,410 --> 01:15:36,439
It's not peeled.

1926
01:15:36,440 --> 01:15:37,510
(She uses a slicer to julienne a pear?)

1927
01:15:37,940 --> 01:15:40,009
Other people make juice out of pears.

1928
01:15:40,010 --> 01:15:41,709
- But I just... - You julienne them.

1929
01:15:41,710 --> 01:15:43,779
The strips fall apart over time.

1930
01:15:43,780 --> 01:15:46,189
- They're soft. So... - Only the refreshing taste is left.

1931
01:15:46,190 --> 01:15:48,719
Right. That's why I don't make juice.

1932
01:15:48,720 --> 01:15:51,519
As the pear softens over time,

1933
01:15:51,520 --> 01:15:53,259
the radish absorbs its sweetness.

1934
01:15:53,260 --> 01:15:55,429
And that makes kimchi better, according to her.

1935
01:15:55,430 --> 01:15:56,800
- Rather than adding sugar? - Yes.

1936
01:15:57,560 --> 01:15:59,429
You can just put some on top of the radish.

1937
01:15:59,430 --> 01:16:01,069
- It will fall on the mat. - Right.

1938
01:16:01,070 --> 01:16:02,699
- Just chop them like this... Yes. - I just have to chop them?

1939
01:16:02,700 --> 01:16:04,870
- Like this. - Oh, okay. Got it.

1940
01:16:05,170 --> 01:16:07,739
I find it hard to believe that this came from the field.

1941
01:16:07,740 --> 01:16:09,740
I'm really grateful.

1942
01:16:10,240 --> 01:16:11,610
The farmer's love.

1943
01:16:12,850 --> 01:16:15,419
(And the mother's technique)

1944
01:16:15,420 --> 01:16:16,550
This is fun.

1945
01:16:17,650 --> 01:16:19,149
She works really fast.

1946
01:16:19,150 --> 01:16:20,150
She's the best.

1947
01:16:20,151 --> 01:16:21,520
It's because she's done it so many times before.

1948
01:16:22,190 --> 01:16:24,820
(KEY works hard to chop it next to her.)

1949
01:16:25,190 --> 01:16:26,330
It's done.

1950
01:16:27,630 --> 01:16:29,699
How did she do it all by herself?

1951
01:16:29,700 --> 01:16:31,430
Exactly. How did she do it all by herself?

1952
01:16:31,530 --> 01:16:33,029
She's like an artisan. She's the kimchi master.

1953
01:16:33,030 --> 01:16:34,329
(That's what makes someone a master.)

1954
01:16:34,330 --> 01:16:37,340
- We should add the sauce, right? - We should mix it with the sauce.

1955
01:16:37,840 --> 01:16:39,270
I can mix it with the sauce.

1956
01:16:39,640 --> 01:16:41,609
I'll go and bring the sauces.

1957
01:16:41,610 --> 01:16:42,640
Okay.

1958
01:16:42,910 --> 01:16:44,610
(Cutting)

1959
01:16:45,910 --> 01:16:48,310
He's working hard quietly.

1960
01:16:48,780 --> 01:16:50,919
- In here? - Spread it evenly.

1961
01:16:50,920 --> 01:16:52,020
Like that? Okay.

1962
01:16:52,590 --> 01:16:54,189
(Pouring)

1963
01:16:54,190 --> 01:16:55,319
Here goes chili powder.

1964
01:16:55,320 --> 01:16:58,020
(The cheery energy is a bonus.)

1965
01:16:59,560 --> 01:17:00,759
Look at the chili powder.

1966
01:17:00,760 --> 01:17:01,860
(The red chili powder covers the vegetables.)

1967
01:17:02,400 --> 01:17:05,200
Here. Pour the whole bottle in here.

1968
01:17:05,260 --> 01:17:07,069
- What is that, ma'am? - It's juice of salted shrimp.

1969
01:17:07,070 --> 01:17:08,100
I see.

1970
01:17:08,430 --> 01:17:10,669
- Salted shrimp. - Just the juice, not the shrimp.

1971
01:17:10,670 --> 01:17:12,340
- Just the juice, not the shrimp? - Right.

1972
01:17:12,640 --> 01:17:13,840
This is plum extract.

1973
01:17:14,240 --> 01:17:16,210
- She adds plum extract too? - That's a common ingredient.

1974
01:17:16,410 --> 01:17:17,810
This is salted anchovy.

1975
01:17:18,280 --> 01:17:20,310
- Anchovy juice. - Pour it.

1976
01:17:21,050 --> 01:17:22,880
(Focused)

1977
01:17:23,450 --> 01:17:24,719
(While preparing the young radish...)

1978
01:17:24,720 --> 01:17:26,150
(Glancing)

1979
01:17:27,790 --> 01:17:29,420
What is that?

1980
01:17:30,220 --> 01:17:31,220
Do it like this.

1981
01:17:31,420 --> 01:17:33,259
(Now that everything is in here, we should mix them together.)

1982
01:17:33,260 --> 01:17:34,560
You're quick at doing this.

1983
01:17:34,930 --> 01:17:37,200
(While glancing at them,)

1984
01:17:38,300 --> 01:17:39,369
(KEY quietly prepares the radish staying at the same spot.)

1985
01:17:39,370 --> 01:17:41,629
- I just worked hard quietly. - That's great.

1986
01:17:41,630 --> 01:17:42,939
That's a lot.

1987
01:17:42,940 --> 01:17:44,070
You look like a factory worker.

1988
01:17:45,170 --> 01:17:47,710
Now I'll add the porridge we made.

1989
01:17:48,740 --> 01:17:51,280
(The kimchi master's key ingredient completes the sauce.)

1990
01:17:51,740 --> 01:17:54,149
- She uses no store-bought products. - That's right.

1991
01:17:54,150 --> 01:17:56,620
She made each and every ingredient.

1992
01:17:56,720 --> 01:17:58,419
Oh, my. She's really incredible.

1993
01:17:58,420 --> 01:17:59,820
I see no store-bought products.

1994
01:18:00,090 --> 01:18:01,819
Oh, dear. It's done.

1995
01:18:01,820 --> 01:18:03,420
(They made kimchi filling with natural ingredients.)

1996
01:18:05,160 --> 01:18:06,189
My, look at the filling.

1997
01:18:06,190 --> 01:18:08,090
- It looks good. - It looks really good.

1998
01:18:08,290 --> 01:18:10,860
(Pushing)

1999
01:18:11,430 --> 01:18:12,969
(He washes young radish without being asked.)

2000
01:18:12,970 --> 01:18:14,170
That's a lot. They'll make kimchi with all that?

2001
01:18:14,500 --> 01:18:16,140
There are so many things to do.

2002
01:18:17,840 --> 01:18:19,309
(He even removes water thoroughly.)

2003
01:18:19,310 --> 01:18:20,410
He's good at it.

2004
01:18:21,110 --> 01:18:23,110
- I think I'm done. - Okay.

2005
01:18:23,940 --> 01:18:26,650
All right. Let's cook the meat first.

2006
01:18:27,850 --> 01:18:30,580
(It's already 10 to 6.)

2007
01:18:31,550 --> 01:18:34,120
(Reaching for the cabbage)

2008
01:18:34,750 --> 01:18:36,320
(Watch closely.)

2009
01:18:36,520 --> 01:18:38,289
Put it only on the stem.

2010
01:18:38,290 --> 01:18:40,060
(The kimchi master's secret: Put the filling only on the stem.)

2011
01:18:40,860 --> 01:18:43,530
(He scans the kimchi once.)

2012
01:18:44,860 --> 01:18:46,770
She does everything thoroughly.

2013
01:18:46,930 --> 01:18:48,299
It shouldn't be just red.

2014
01:18:48,300 --> 01:18:49,640
It shouldn't be too red.

2015
01:18:50,640 --> 01:18:52,240
(Pressing)

2016
01:18:53,310 --> 01:18:54,570
Look at that. She's really fast.

2017
01:18:54,640 --> 01:18:55,940
She's really fast.

2018
01:18:56,540 --> 01:18:58,780
(Rubbing)

2019
01:19:00,050 --> 01:19:01,549
(KEY speeds up to catch up with the master.)

2020
01:19:01,550 --> 01:19:03,650
It was my first time making that much kimchi at once.

2021
01:19:04,680 --> 01:19:06,120
I shouldn't be doing this.

2022
01:19:06,890 --> 01:19:09,490
(KEY finished one head as well.)

2023
01:19:09,690 --> 01:19:12,059
Big ones are done more quickly.

2024
01:19:12,060 --> 01:19:13,660
Ma'am, how do I wrap this?

2025
01:19:14,030 --> 01:19:15,390
With the outermost leaf.

2026
01:19:15,960 --> 01:19:17,300
Yes. Fold it first.

2027
01:19:17,460 --> 01:19:18,929
So that the filling doesn't fall out.

2028
01:19:18,930 --> 01:19:20,900
- Oh, that's why. - Yes.

2029
01:19:22,870 --> 01:19:23,970
Like this, right?

2030
01:19:24,270 --> 01:19:25,740
- I should put the filling on it. - Yes.

2031
01:19:26,940 --> 01:19:29,040
Okay. We can do it.

2032
01:19:29,240 --> 01:19:30,310
Yes!

2033
01:19:31,010 --> 01:19:32,110
Let's do this!

2034
01:19:32,610 --> 01:19:34,280
(We've got this!)

2035
01:19:35,210 --> 01:19:36,579
(With a shout,)

2036
01:19:36,580 --> 01:19:37,880
(they cheer up to make all napa cabbages red.)

2037
01:19:39,390 --> 01:19:42,660
(Kimchi, kimchi, ya, ya, ya)

2038
01:19:45,320 --> 01:19:47,460
(We'll make kimchi with all of those?)

2039
01:19:48,160 --> 01:19:49,529
This is really...

2040
01:19:49,530 --> 01:19:51,030
(Gasping)

2041
01:19:52,530 --> 01:19:55,840
(More)

2042
01:19:56,540 --> 01:19:59,970
(The more napa cabbages are brought, the less they speak.)

2043
01:20:01,370 --> 01:20:02,839
(They even season their clothes too.)

2044
01:20:02,840 --> 01:20:04,339
- Look at her clothes. - I got it all over my clothes.

2045
01:20:04,340 --> 01:20:05,750
- It happens with no exception. - I see.

2046
01:20:06,550 --> 01:20:09,350
I should put them in the containers.

2047
01:20:10,680 --> 01:20:11,720
Oh, dear.

2048
01:20:12,250 --> 01:20:14,490
Oh, my. You both did a great job.

2049
01:20:15,020 --> 01:20:16,090
Oh, dear.

2050
01:20:16,890 --> 01:20:19,459
I want to give her a massage after this...

2051
01:20:19,460 --> 01:20:21,560
because she worked so hard.

2052
01:20:22,630 --> 01:20:24,260
I feel so sorry.

2053
01:20:24,600 --> 01:20:25,700
She's so nice.

2054
01:20:26,730 --> 01:20:28,069
She has a warm heart.

2055
01:20:28,070 --> 01:20:29,999
I know. That's why I don't call her...

2056
01:20:30,000 --> 01:20:31,639
I don't call her my mother-in-law.

2057
01:20:31,640 --> 01:20:32,840
- You call her your mom. - She's my mom.

2058
01:20:33,440 --> 01:20:35,470
She's really... You know, that's...

2059
01:20:36,180 --> 01:20:40,209
While making kimchi, I think she became serious.

2060
01:20:40,210 --> 01:20:42,449
She thought it'd be simple and easy...

2061
01:20:42,450 --> 01:20:45,120
to make the filling and put it between leaves.

2062
01:20:45,250 --> 01:20:46,489
She had no idea...

2063
01:20:46,490 --> 01:20:47,850
- it would be this hard. - Of course.

2064
01:20:48,290 --> 01:20:49,690
- Is there anything else? - No.

2065
01:20:50,220 --> 01:20:51,559
- Are you sure? - No.

2066
01:20:51,560 --> 01:20:52,989
- It's done? - Oh, my gosh.

2067
01:20:52,990 --> 01:20:55,059
- Yes! - You both did a great job.

2068
01:20:55,060 --> 01:20:56,300
Great job, everyone.

2069
01:20:57,500 --> 01:20:58,560
Dear me.

2070
01:20:59,670 --> 01:21:01,030
Gosh, my back.

2071
01:21:01,970 --> 01:21:04,800
Oh, my legs! My everything!

2072
01:21:05,400 --> 01:21:07,409
Everything. My whole body is aching.

2073
01:21:07,410 --> 01:21:08,540
We made a lot of kimchi.

2074
01:21:09,110 --> 01:21:12,650
Kany, let's eat bossam so that you can go home.

2075
01:21:12,780 --> 01:21:13,780
- Really? - Yes.

2076
01:21:13,781 --> 01:21:14,879
Yes! Great.

2077
01:21:14,880 --> 01:21:16,880
(Really? Yes!)

2078
01:21:18,680 --> 01:21:19,720
- Oh, my. - Look at that.

2079
01:21:20,290 --> 01:21:21,549
But that was fun.

2080
01:21:21,550 --> 01:21:23,859
(They finally finished making kimchi with 70 heads of napa cabbage.)

2081
01:21:23,860 --> 01:21:25,459
I'm going to eat a lot.

2082
01:21:25,460 --> 01:21:26,760
(With young radish kimchi, there's a lot of kimchi here.)

2083
01:21:27,560 --> 01:21:29,359
- How did you do all that? - You used all 70 heads.

2084
01:21:29,360 --> 01:21:30,500
In one day.

2085
01:21:32,730 --> 01:21:34,099
(KEY is exhausted...)

2086
01:21:34,100 --> 01:21:35,429
(from making kimchi with 70 heads of napa cabbage in 1 day.)

2087
01:21:35,430 --> 01:21:38,040
(Both KEY and Kany are exhausted.)

2088
01:21:38,870 --> 01:21:41,610
Kany, here's oyster bossam for you.

2089
01:21:42,170 --> 01:21:43,939
Hold on. I'll make the sauce quickly.

2090
01:21:43,940 --> 01:21:45,579
Kany's mother-in-law has the highest stamina among the three.

2091
01:21:45,580 --> 01:21:46,580
- Exactly. - That's right.

2092
01:21:46,581 --> 01:21:48,210
She doesn't even seem tired.

2093
01:21:48,710 --> 01:21:49,780
Oh, my gosh.

2094
01:21:50,120 --> 01:21:51,420
- My, homemade food. - It looks good.

2095
01:21:53,050 --> 01:21:55,150
(Well-cooked suyuk)

2096
01:21:55,920 --> 01:21:58,320
(And today's star, kimchi)

2097
01:21:59,190 --> 01:22:01,090
(Fresh raw oyster)

2098
01:22:03,030 --> 01:22:04,030
(Chewy half-dried saury)

2099
01:22:04,031 --> 01:22:05,969
You can't go wrong with half-dried saury.

2100
01:22:05,970 --> 01:22:07,399
She also made acorn jelly.

2101
01:22:07,400 --> 01:22:08,599
(Acorn jelly that she made with her own hands)

2102
01:22:08,600 --> 01:22:10,740
(And her own secret sauce)

2103
01:22:12,040 --> 01:22:14,170
- Nothing here is from a store. - That's right.

2104
01:22:14,470 --> 01:22:15,740
How is that possible?

2105
01:22:15,940 --> 01:22:18,210
- Making them alone must be tiring. - She's really incredible.

2106
01:22:18,580 --> 01:22:21,710
Try it with some oysters. And try the half-dried saury too.

2107
01:22:22,110 --> 01:22:23,850
- Didn't I bring... Oh, it's here. - Here.

2108
01:22:27,950 --> 01:22:31,559
(The touching moment of having his first bite)

2109
01:22:31,560 --> 01:22:32,590
Is it good?

2110
01:22:35,160 --> 01:22:36,860
(Amazed)

2111
01:22:38,730 --> 01:22:39,899
Really?

2112
01:22:39,900 --> 01:22:42,369
(She makes a vegetable wrap carefully with an oyster.)

2113
01:22:42,370 --> 01:22:43,540
Did you put it in there?

2114
01:22:44,240 --> 01:22:46,440
- They're enjoying winter food. - This is killing me.

2115
01:22:48,210 --> 01:22:49,310
I wonder if it's tasty.

2116
01:22:52,510 --> 01:22:54,110
- I'll cut it for you with my hands. - Okay.

2117
01:22:54,410 --> 01:22:55,410
Thank you.

2118
01:22:56,050 --> 01:22:58,580
You're supposed to tear kimchi like this. This is the right way.

2119
01:22:58,850 --> 01:23:01,549
- It tastes so good. - Korean food is popular globally.

2120
01:23:01,550 --> 01:23:03,219
And you can't enjoy that taste anywhere else in the world.

2121
01:23:03,220 --> 01:23:04,619
- You're right. - You can't find that.

2122
01:23:04,620 --> 01:23:06,930
- You can enjoy it only in Korea. - That's right.

2123
01:23:07,230 --> 01:23:08,630
- You... - Mom, please eat.

2124
01:23:10,560 --> 01:23:12,970
(Shall I?)

2125
01:23:15,700 --> 01:23:17,099
- Why? - Too much.

2126
01:23:17,100 --> 01:23:18,740
(She finally has a bite.)

2127
01:23:18,870 --> 01:23:20,240
- Thank you. - Have that piece.

2128
01:23:20,610 --> 01:23:21,669
Gosh, this is...

2129
01:23:21,670 --> 01:23:23,710
(He picks up a strip of freshly made kimchi.)

2130
01:23:24,140 --> 01:23:25,240
That looks crazy good.

2131
01:23:26,450 --> 01:23:27,479
Oh, my.

2132
01:23:27,480 --> 01:23:29,650
(He puts everything in his mouth.)

2133
01:23:31,880 --> 01:23:34,250
(The joy that came after long, hard work)

2134
01:23:36,120 --> 01:23:37,589
(The fantastic combination)

2135
01:23:37,590 --> 01:23:40,130
(Mother's acorn jelly and her secret sauce)

2136
01:23:40,990 --> 01:23:43,229
That sauce was crazy good.

2137
01:23:43,230 --> 01:23:44,900
She must've made it deliciously.

2138
01:23:45,730 --> 01:23:46,730
Oh, my.

2139
01:23:47,200 --> 01:23:48,730
I visit my friends on my days off...

2140
01:23:49,800 --> 01:23:52,470
and help them make kimchi, which I haven't done before.

2141
01:23:52,770 --> 01:23:56,280
Or I invite a friend over and cook for him or her.

2142
01:23:56,580 --> 01:24:00,579
That's my own way of showing my love for them.

2143
01:24:00,580 --> 01:24:02,079
It's to let them know that I care about them.

2144
01:24:02,080 --> 01:24:04,120
It's to express how much I adore them.

2145
01:24:04,380 --> 01:24:06,550
Rather than just saying it to them,

2146
01:24:07,020 --> 01:24:08,649
I want to show my affection through my actions...

2147
01:24:08,650 --> 01:24:11,059
and hear what they think.

2148
01:24:11,060 --> 01:24:13,060
I believe it's a good way of communicating with others.

2149
01:24:14,130 --> 01:24:15,290
All right. Now...

2150
01:24:15,800 --> 01:24:17,800
Kany, you should go home and rest too. You worked hard.

2151
01:24:17,900 --> 01:24:18,929
- You worked really hard. - You too.

2152
01:24:18,930 --> 01:24:21,070
I'll stay a little longer and eat some more.

2153
01:24:22,900 --> 01:24:25,699
- Take your time and eat a lot. - Right. Eat a lot.

2154
01:24:25,700 --> 01:24:26,909
(Kimchi Conveys Love)

2155
01:24:26,910 --> 01:24:28,440
(Next story)

2156
01:24:28,910 --> 01:24:31,139
(Someone is cleaning his room like crazy.)

2157
01:24:31,140 --> 01:24:32,280
(Is he Kang Yong?)

2158
01:24:33,050 --> 01:24:34,080
(What time is it?)

2159
01:24:34,310 --> 01:24:35,850
(He crams his clothes in his closet hastily.)

2160
01:24:36,480 --> 01:24:37,980
(He's covering the mess.)

2161
01:24:38,620 --> 01:24:40,390
(I can finish this in 1 minute.)

2162
01:24:40,990 --> 01:24:42,220
(A very important person for me...)

2163
01:24:42,350 --> 01:24:43,920
(is visiting my house after a long time.)

2164
01:24:44,490 --> 01:24:46,430
(So I cleaned the house hastily and prepared to welcome her.)

2165
01:24:47,260 --> 01:24:48,289
(The important person for him is...)

2166
01:24:48,290 --> 01:24:50,800
(Kang Yong's mother!)

2167
01:24:51,830 --> 01:24:52,870
(The mother and son, who look alike, meet each other.)

2168
01:24:53,330 --> 01:24:54,330
(My house is clean and tidy, right?)

2169
01:24:54,770 --> 01:24:56,070
(Smiling)

2170
01:24:56,570 --> 01:24:58,340
(You should vacuum here again.)

2171
01:24:59,000 --> 01:25:00,640
(Can you give it a quick wipe?)

2172
01:25:00,870 --> 01:25:02,710
(He did his best to clean the house, but his mother cleans it again.)

2173
01:25:03,080 --> 01:25:07,350
(He keeps saying, "I'll do it.")

2174
01:25:08,510 --> 01:25:09,619
(He tries to distract her.)

2175
01:25:09,620 --> 01:25:10,820
(How about you have a walnut cake instead of cleaning?)

2176
01:25:11,180 --> 01:25:12,650
(I won an award too.)

2177
01:25:13,590 --> 01:25:14,590
(They make good memories.)

2178
01:25:14,591 --> 01:25:15,890
(And he successfully distracts her.)

2179
01:25:16,090 --> 01:25:17,260
(He kills 2 birds with 1 stone.)

2180
01:25:17,820 --> 01:25:19,859
(She brought a gift for her loving son.)

2181
01:25:19,860 --> 01:25:21,490
(A lucky broom with sensitive girl vibes)

2182
01:25:21,860 --> 01:25:22,860
(Shall I hang it here?)

2183
01:25:23,160 --> 01:25:25,200
(I'll hang it myself, my son.)

2184
01:25:25,800 --> 01:25:27,470
(Son! Can you give me a cup of tea?)

2185
01:25:27,700 --> 01:25:28,930
(Tea?)

2186
01:25:29,370 --> 01:25:30,770
(Chuckling)

2187
01:25:31,000 --> 01:25:32,570
(I brought floral coasters too.)

2188
01:25:33,070 --> 01:25:34,610
(His house is becoming pretty?)

2189
01:25:35,310 --> 01:25:37,110
(Wait. This isn't right.)

2190
01:25:38,410 --> 01:25:40,450
(The mother and son, who look alike, have warm hearts.)

2191
01:25:41,150 --> 01:25:42,179
(Salute!)

2192
01:25:42,180 --> 01:25:43,480
(MINHO salutes someone?)

2193
01:25:44,250 --> 01:25:45,620
(At ease)

2194
01:25:46,120 --> 01:25:48,820
(They're MINHO's fellow marines.)

2195
01:25:49,790 --> 01:25:50,989
(Salute!)

2196
01:25:50,990 --> 01:25:53,090
(I am Choi Min Ho, the 1,245th batch.)

2197
01:25:53,860 --> 01:25:55,260
(With those who were his senior and junior in the military...)

2198
01:25:56,030 --> 01:25:57,699
(To end 2025 well,)

2199
01:25:57,700 --> 01:25:59,300
(we went to Gangwon Province.)

2200
01:25:59,930 --> 01:26:01,770
(Is it 11km? That's a piece of cake.)

2201
01:26:02,500 --> 01:26:03,700
(They're going to climb up a mountain in winter.)

2202
01:26:04,440 --> 01:26:06,440
(They prepare themselves thoroughly, tightening their combat boots.)

2203
01:26:06,640 --> 01:26:09,010
(Once a marine, always a marine.)

2204
01:26:09,440 --> 01:26:10,840
(Won't your ears be cold?)

2205
01:26:11,140 --> 01:26:12,479
(I can overcome it with my passion.)

2206
01:26:12,480 --> 01:26:14,510
(If they're cold, I'll cut off one of them.)

2207
01:26:14,850 --> 01:26:16,420
(Wait for us, winter mountain.)

2208
01:26:16,750 --> 01:26:18,949
(Ghost-catching marines)

2209
01:26:18,950 --> 01:26:19,950
(Shouting)

2210
01:26:20,020 --> 01:26:22,990
(We'll run right up to the top of the mountain!)

2211
01:26:23,590 --> 01:26:24,690
(Slurping)

2212
01:26:24,790 --> 01:26:26,530
(Because we have to eat cup ramyeon.)

2213
01:26:27,260 --> 01:26:28,359
(It's basically a ramyeon eating challenge.)

2214
01:26:28,360 --> 01:26:30,200
(We climbed up here for this.)

2215
01:26:30,860 --> 01:26:34,569
(Ramyeon tastes so good on top of the mountain.)

2216
01:26:34,570 --> 01:26:36,000
I feel like I'm back in time.

2217
01:26:36,270 --> 01:26:39,040
(This why we climb up a mountain in winter.)

2218
01:26:39,970 --> 01:26:42,709
(The taste they will enjoy next is...)

2219
01:26:42,710 --> 01:26:44,540
(a sleigh ride!)

2220
01:26:44,880 --> 01:26:46,010
(It's exciting.)

2221
01:26:46,450 --> 01:26:49,380
(We have to make a bet.)

2222
01:26:49,780 --> 01:26:52,050
(Their pride as former marines is on the line.)

2223
01:26:52,380 --> 01:26:53,789
(Are you ready?)

2224
01:26:53,790 --> 01:26:54,790
(Screaming)

2225
01:26:54,950 --> 01:26:56,690
(Let's go.)

2226
01:26:58,060 --> 01:27:01,990
(This Is the Taste of Winter)
